    "judgment": {
      "status": "confirmed",
      "reasoning": "All 16 mathematical answers agree with their own captured Math Academy worked solutions. q-136312: speed is sqrt(1²+4²)=sqrt(17), rounded to 4.12 km/h. q-136341: v=u+at=(-1,4)+6(2,1)=(11,10) km/h. q-136289: a=(v-u)/4=((2,0)-(-2,-8))/4=(1,2) m/s². q-136291: both component equations, -7+3t=2 and -6+2t=0, give t=3 s. q-47854: the uncancelled denominator vanishes at x=sqrt(2), producing the sole vertical asymptote. q-68167: cancellation gives x/6 for x≠3/2, so the excluded point is a hole. q-107068: cancellation gives x²+3x-5 for x≠-4,0, so both excluded points are holes. In those two solutions, 'the denominator is never zero' applies to the simplified expression; the original domain exclusions remain. q-107074: both denominators are strictly positive for real x, so there are no vertical asymptotes. q-142026: the sine addition formula with cos(7π/6)=-sqrt(3)/2 and sin(7π/6)=-1/2 gives the selected expression. q-76067: intersecting x≥-6 and x≥-2 gives [-2,∞). q-105436: intersecting x≥-4 and x≥-6 gives [-4,∞). Radical endpoints are included with continuity relative to the domain. q-38831: cancellation does not restore the excluded point x=0. q-4561: the denominator excludes x=-2. q-76071: 3+2^x>0, so the logarithm requires 2x+2>0, giving x>-1. q-39765: the rational inner function excludes x=±1, and sine is continuous elsewhere. q-40081: cosecant excludes -π, 0, and π from the specified interval. Evidence here establishes agreement with the captured worked solutions; no submitted-answer grades were supplied.",
